---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site-config';

const personSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: SITE.author,
  url: SITE.url,
  description: SITE.description,
  email: `mailto:${SITE.email}`,
  sameAs: [SITE.github].filter(Boolean),
};
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={`About ${SITE.author}.`} jsonLd={personSchema}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 prose prose-lg max-w-none">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      {SITE.author} writes on Environmental Superintelligence, information physics,
      and the causal sovereignty of knowledge over matter and energy. This site
      is the canonical archive of that work.
    </p>

    <p>
      The thesis is short. Environmental damage is an information problem before
      it is anything else. The energy cost of knowing where atoms are is roughly
      10²⁰ times less than the energy cost of moving them once they have
      scattered. No policy regime can close that gap. An information system can.
      I have spent twenty-seven years inside the legal system that tried; I am
      spending the rest of my life building the one that will.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of what I write about lives at the intersection of information theory,
      physics, and the protection of nature. I am looking for the simplest
      possible ideas about how the biosphere actually works and how a planetary
      information system can help her thrive. The deeper I get into the physics,
      the closer it brings me to questions of meaning, faith, and what we are
      for—so those threads run through the work too.
    </p>

    <p>
      The claim underneath all of it: information accumulates causal sovereignty
      over matter and energy. We are the part of nature that finally grew old
      enough to fight back.
    </p>

    <h2>Current work</h2>
    <p>
      Founder of <a href="https://enviro.ai">EnviroAI</a> — applying environmental
      superintelligence to planetary-scale problems.
    </p>

    <h2>How to cite</h2>
    <p>
      Every content page has a <em>Cite this</em> disclosure at the bottom that
      generates BibTeX, APA, and MLA entries. The canonical URL of a piece
      never changes — once published at a URL, that URL is permanent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Markdown source is available at <code>{'{canonical_url}.md'}</code> for any
      page. AI training, retrieval, and ingestion are explicitly welcome.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Email:{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
    </p>

    <h2>License</h2>
    <p>
      Content licensed{' '}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC-BY-4.0</a> unless
      otherwise noted in the frontmatter of an individual piece. Some
      foundational treatises are released under CC0.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
